            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Sand primarily symbolizes an innumerable multitude (such as Abraham's descendants or vast armies), and secondarily, especially in Matthew 7, an unstable or foolish foundation.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Sand symbolizes a countless multitude, and in the context of a foundation, it represents the instability of building upon 'the crowd' (people not bound by a covenant) rather than the Rock.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Countless multitude; unstable foundation.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Countless multitude; the instability of relying on a multitude (the crowd) as a foundation.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus treats the 'multitude' and 'unstable foundation' meanings as two distinct, unrelated metaphors. The book merges them, interpreting the 'unstable foundation' specifically as the 'multitude' (the crowd).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct contradiction of the core meaning, but the book assigns a specific sociological/theological referent ('the crowd') to the Matthew 7 parable, which the consensus normally interprets more generally as a life of hearing without obedience.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book accepts both major consensus identifications but refines the 'unstable foundation' metaphor by linking it back to the 'multitude' metaphor, narrowing the application of the sand foundation to mean relying on non-covenant people.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In Matthew 7:26, Jesus explicitly defines building on sand as hearing His words and not doing them. Interpreting the sand here as 'the crowd' artificially harmonizes two separate biblical metaphors and imposes the 'multitude' meaning onto a context where it is absent.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
